In the beginning, the Forest of Aethel was divided by a chasm of cold stone. Two spirits, Kael the Gatherer and Mira the Well-Keeper, stood on opposite cliffs. Kael held the seeds of golden wheat, yet the soil of his cliff was barren dust. Mira possessed the crystalline waters of the deep spring, yet she hungered for the sustenance of grain. They lived in silent isolation, watching the seasons wither their hopes.

One cycle, Kael dared to cast his pouch across the chasm. The wind caught the seeds, delivering them to Mira. Seeing the offering, trust blossomed like a desert flower. Mira did not hoard the grain; instead, she nurtured it with the freshest waters of her spring. The magic of the soil, fueled by her generosity, multiplied the harvest tenfold. A bridge of emerald vines sprouted across the chasm, linking their spirits forever. They learned that the act of giving was not a loss, but a multiplier of life. To withhold was to starve, but to extend a hand was to build a future. Thus, the law of the forest was written in the soil: what is shared returneth in abundance, sustaining both the giver and the taker in a dance of grace.

